<!--Post: 9499--><!--Time: 1109539388-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9499"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 27, 2005 - 1:23 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Okay...I&#39;m dropping the dime on my silly ass self, but one of my nicks at Ks.com was Lrry_Andrsn, or Larry Anderson.  <BR> <BR>I liked being Larry. He was, as one fellow poster there suggested, a sort of Tom Bodette, Motel 6 sort of fella. But for some reason, as Larry, I channelled some good profiling, and I swear to God I didn&#39;t even remember it till today. Here&#39;s <a href="http://forums.prospero.com/kr-kansas_news/messages?msg=4975.30">the link to one entry I made on Jan. 3</a>, and the best quote from it. I was communing with the freakin&#39; spirits or something. Writing as Larry I said, in the post linked <b>&#40;what I seem to have nailed is in bold&#41;</b>: <BR> <BR>&#34;...Hey, la-la-la-la Lola, Larry Anderson responding; <BR> <BR><b>In daily life I&#39;d suspect BTK to be very unassuming, in the opinions of those around him. However, I think a close examination of his social sphere, even his family, would show a history of people &#39;getting bad vibes&#39;, or feeling that he could be inappropriate. Not in the sense of taking liberties...I&#39;ve said before, this doesn&#39;t seem like a guy who would ever get in trouble for something as alpha male as say groping a woman...he&#39;d be more likely to say off-color things. He&#39;d be the guy who takes a lewd, or sick joke too far. He will probably have a history of &#39;pushing it&#39; with those whom he is comfortable with. He is the kind of person who I think may make people around him uncomfortable in a way they can&#39;t ever pin down, and I bet a lot of them feel guilty for feeling that way. Because I also feel he may seem altruistic to those whom he&#39;s worked with and socialized with, say, at church...&#34;</b> <i>&#40;comment: based on stuff I read today, and late last night, most of this is describing Dennis Rader fairly well. Don&#39;t know about him being &#34;off-color&#34; yet, though.&#41;</i>  <BR> <BR>Later in the post, I wrote: <BR> <BR>&#34;...<b>I think there&#39;s even a chance that he&#39;s the kind of man who people rarely speak ill of, yet they can&#39;t find a reason to speak too highly of him either, and they may admit privately they are always kind of glad when he leaves.</b> I feel he does a lot of smirking. His own exploits amuse him endlessly, and provide him with something interesting to get through the day...&#34; <i>&#40;don&#39;t know about the last part, but I&#39;m betting it&#39;s true.&#41;</i> <BR> <BR>More... <BR> <BR>&#34;...<b>I believe a couple of interesting things about his appearance; he does have a mustache, and has probably worn one for years, and he wears glasses. The frames will be larger than actually necessary for his face, and heavy. They will make him appear almost jowly, even if his weight is normal. He will be known, too, by some, like women whom he has worked with, as a starer. Always polite, perhaps, accomodating, but very watchful, intent...</b>&#34; <BR> <BR>I read an article today I&#39;m going to have to go back and find where someone is quoted as saying Rader tended to &#34;stare.&#34; <BR> <BR>I emphasize, <b>I got plenty of crap wrong, too.</b> <BR> <BR>But I&#39;ll admit, some of the stuff I got right gives me the willies. My picture of the man in my head was incredibly vivid, and it was when I finally saw Rader&#39;s mug shot that I said, that&#39;s basically the kind of guy I was seeing in my mind&#39;s eye. Not saying any psychic crap was going on, more something instinctive.  <BR> <BR>It may have helped that I have a 60-something, heavyset, former Air Force Chief Master Sergeant Father who retired from being a Nashville Electrical <b>Codes</b> Inspector about 5 years ago. My Dad&#39;s actually a sweetheart, but I felt I should note the commonalities, tenuous though they may be.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
